  user_input {
    user_response: "You are modifying an EXISTING website called CLIMATELENS.\n\nIMPORTANT: DO NOT BUILD A NEW WEBSITE.\n\nYour job is ONLY to redesign, reorganize, clean up, and improve the EXISTING ClimateLens website while preserving its existing functionality and project structure.\n\nBefore making ANY changes:\n\n1. Inspect the entire existing project.\n2. Understand the current pages, components, routes, state management, data flow, Gemini/API integration, charts, PDF handling, verification logic, and existing functionality.\n3. Identify which components are already functional.\n4. Reuse existing components and logic wherever possible.\n5. Do not replace working functionality with fake UI or static demonstrations.\n6. Do not remove existing features just because the UI is being redesigned.\n\n==================================================\nCORE PRODUCT IDEA\n==================================================\n\nClimateLens converts complex corporate environmental reports into structured, understandable and reviewable environmental information.\n\nThe visual story of the website must be:\n\nMESSY REPORT\n      \342\206\223\nAI EXTRACTION\n      \342\206\223\nSTANDARDIZATION\n      \342\206\223\nDATA CHECK\n      \342\206\223\nCLEAN DATA\n      \342\206\223\nVERIFICATION\n      \342\206\223\nENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT\n      \342\206\223\nFINAL REPORT\n\nThe website should make this transformation visually obvious.\n\n==================================================\nMOST IMPORTANT DESIGN RULE\n==================================================\n\nTHE WEBSITE ITSELF MUST BE CLEAN.\n\nDo NOT make every page look like a complicated dashboard.\n\nThere should be ONLY ONE intentionally dense area:\n\nRAW REPORT / RAW DATA\n\nThat area should visually represent the huge amount of messy information found inside corporate environmental reports.\n\nEverything after that should progressively become cleaner.\n\nThink:\n\nRAW REPORT = messy and information-heavy\n\nPROCESSING = structured transformation\n\nCLEAN DATA = simple\n\nVERIFICATION = focused\n\nFINAL REPORT = extremely clean\n\n==================================================\nVISUAL STYLE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a professional environmental intelligence platform.\n\nUse:\n\n- deep charcoal / near-black background\n- white and light-gray typography\n- green as the main environmental accent\n- cyan/blue for informational elements\n- amber for warnings\n- red only for important/critical flags\n- subtle borders\n- subtle shadows\n- rounded but professional components\n- generous whitespace\n- strong typography hierarchy\n\nThe visual feeling should be:\n\nEnvironmental Intelligence Platform\n+\nProfessional Data Analytics\n+\nModern Enterprise SaaS\n\nDo NOT make it:\n\n- cyberpunk\n- gaming style\n- neon-heavy\n- overly futuristic\n- excessively animated\n- visually noisy\n- full of unnecessary cards\n\nUse animation only where it helps explain the data transformation.\n\n==================================================\nNAVIGATION\n==================================================\n\nSimplify the existing navigation.\n\nUse:\n\nCLIMATELENS\n\nOverview\n\nReports\n  Upload\n  Raw Data\n  Clean Data\n  History\n\nVerification\n  Checks\n  Flags\n  Environmental Context\n\nFinal Reports\n\nSettings\n\nDo not create unnecessary pages.\n\nIf equivalent existing pages already exist, reorganize them rather than creating duplicate pages.\n\n==================================================\nOVERVIEW PAGE\n==================================================\n\nRedesign the existing Overview page to be very clean.\n\nAt the top show only four important metrics:\n\nREPORTS PROCESSED\nDATA POINTS\nAI CONFIDENCE\nNEEDS REVIEW\n\nUse the application\'s actual data/state.\n\nBelow that, place ONE large primary environmental trend graph.\n\nAllow the existing data to control the graph.\n\nPossible metrics:\n\n- Emissions\n- Energy\n- Water\n- Waste\n\nDo not show all graphs at once.\n\nLet the user switch between metrics.\n\nBelow the graph, show a short AI/data insight.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Reported emissions increased 22% compared with the previous period.\"\n\nThen show only the most important review items.\n\nDo not fill the Overview page with dozens of cards.\n\nThe Overview should answer:\n\nWhat has been processed?\nWhat was extracted?\nHow reliable is the extraction?\nWhat needs attention?\n\n==================================================\nREPORT UPLOAD\n==================================================\n\nKeep the existing upload functionality.\n\nMake the upload interface extremely simple.\n\nShow:\n\nUpload Report\n\nSupported document:\nPDF\n\nThen show the processing state clearly:\n\nUploading\n\342\206\223\nReading document\n\342\206\223\nExtracting environmental data\n\342\206\223\nStandardizing values\n\342\206\223\nChecking data\n\342\206\223\nReady for review\n\nDo not pretend processing is happening if it is not actually happening.\n\nUse the existing Gemini/API functionality where available.\n\n==================================================\nRAW REPORT / RAW DATA\n==================================================\n\nTHIS IS THE ONLY SECTION THAT SHOULD INTENTIONALLY LOOK DENSE.\n\nIts purpose is to demonstrate the problem ClimateLens solves.\n\nTitle:\n\nRAW REPORT\n\nSubtitle:\n\nComplex environmental information extracted from the source report.\n\nShow realistic dense information such as:\n\n- Scope 1\n- Scope 2\n- Scope 3\n- CO\342\202\202 emissions\n- energy consumption\n- electricity\n- fuel\n- water withdrawal\n- water consumption\n- wastewater\n- waste generated\n- recycled waste\n- renewable energy\n- production volume\n- reporting periods\n- units\n- methodology\n- reporting boundaries\n- footnotes\n- source references\n- page numbers\n- tables\n- notes\n- repeated metrics\n- different terminology\n\nExample summary:\n\n184 Pages\n1,248 Data Points\n37 Tables\n96 Environmental Values\n\nMake this area visually information-heavy.\n\nHowever, it must still be organized and readable.\n\nClearly label demo/sample data if the data is synthetic.\n\nDo NOT make fake data look like real corporate data.\n\n==================================================\nTHE TRANSFORMATION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a strong visual transition from the raw report to the clean dataset.\n\nShow:\n\nRAW REPORT\n\342\206\223\nAI EXTRACTION\n\342\206\223\nSTANDARDIZATION\n\342\206\223\nDATA CHECK\n\342\206\223\nCLEAN DATA\n\nEach step should be visually simple.\n\nFor example:\n\nAI EXTRACTION\nIdentifies environmental values\n\nSTANDARDIZATION\nConverts units and terminology\n\nDATA CHECK\nChecks consistency and completeness\n\nCLEAN DATA\nCreates structured environmental data\n\nDo not use long explanations.\n\n==================================================\nCLEAN DATA\n==================================================\n\nThis section should look dramatically cleaner than Raw Data.\n\nUse a simple structured table.\n\nColumns:\n\nMetric | Value | Unit | Status\n\nExample:\n\nEmissions | 8,420 | tCO\342\202\202e | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nEnergy | 12,450 | MWh | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nWater | 84,200 | KL | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nWaste | 2,840 | tonnes | \342\232\240 Review\n\nUse actual application data where possible.\n\nDo not show every technical field by default.\n\nWhen the user clicks a metric, show additional information:\n\nSource\nPage\nSection\nConfidence\nReporting Period\nMethodology\nReview Status\n\nThis keeps the main table clean while preserving traceability.\n\n==================================================\nGRAPHS\n==================================================\n\nGraphs must become an important visual identity of ClimateLens.\n\nBUT:\n\nDo not add graphs just to make the website look impressive.\n\nEvery graph must communicate useful information.\n\nUse:\n\nLINE GRAPH\nFor historical trends.\n\nBAR GRAPH\nFor category comparisons.\n\nSMALL TREND GRAPH\nFor summary metrics.\n\nCOMPARISON GRAPH\nFor company vs regional environmental indicators.\n\nVERIFICATION GRAPH\nFor highlighting unusual changes.\n\nUse one consistent chart design throughout the application.\n\nDo not use completely different chart styles on every page.\n\nUse the actual application data.\n\n==================================================\nHISTORICAL ANALYSIS\n==================================================\n\nRedesign the existing historical page.\n\nMake the main element a large clean trend graph.\n\nExample:\n\n2023 \342\206\222 2024 \342\206\222 2025 \342\206\222 2026\n\nAllow the user to switch between:\n\n- emissions\n- energy\n- water\n- waste\n- production\n- carbon intensity\n\nIf methodology or reporting boundaries changed, clearly show that as context.\n\nDo not automatically interpret every increase/decrease as suspicious.\n\nUse wording such as:\n\n\"Significant historical change detected.\"\n\nThen explain possible reasons:\n\n- production change\n- reporting boundary change\n- methodology change\n- operational change\n\n==================================================\nVERIFICATION CENTER\n==================================================\n\nKeep the existing verification functionality.\n\nMake the page focused.\n\nShow three simple statuses:\n\n\342\234\223 CONSISTENT\n\n\342\234\223 CHECKED\n\n\342\232\240 NEEDS REVIEW\n\nThen show the most important verification findings.\n\nExample:\n\nScope 1 Emissions\n\n8,420 tCO\342\202\202e\n\nPrevious period:\n6,900 tCO\342\202\202e\n\nChange:\n+22%\n\nStatus:\nNeeds Review\n\nShow the historical graph with the affected point highlighted.\n\nAdd:\n\nINVESTIGATE\n\nWhen clicked, open a side panel/modal.\n\nThe side panel should show:\n\nWhy was this flagged?\n\nWhat values were compared?\n\nWhat source was used?\n\nWhat possible explanations exist?\n\nWhat should the reviewer check?\n\nDo not state that a company has committed fraud or falsified data.\n\nClimateLens identifies potential inconsistencies for human review.\n\n==================================================\nENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nThis page must clearly demonstrate one of ClimateLens\' important capabilities.\n\nShow two trends on the same graph:\n\nCORPORATE TREND\n\nREGIONAL ENVIRONMENTAL INDICATOR\n\nExample:\n\nCompany emissions \342\206\22318%\n\nRegional indicator \342\206\2219%\n\nThen show a concise message:\n\nCONTEXTUAL DIFFERENCE DETECTED\n\n\"Corporate reported emissions moved differently from the selected regional environmental indicator during the comparable period.\"\n\nThen:\n\n\"Further review recommended.\"\n\nAlways include:\n\n\"Correlation does not establish causation.\"\n\nDo not imply that a regional environmental change proves the company\'s reported data is wrong.\n\nKeep this section visual, not text-heavy.\n\n==================================================\nFLAGS\n==================================================\n\nMake Flags very clean.\n\nEach flag should show:\n\nFlag ID\nMetric\nChange\nSeverity\nStatus\n\nExample:\n\nCL-0042\n\nScope 1 Emissions\n\n+22%\n\nNeeds Review\n\nClicking the flag opens detailed information.\n\nDo not show huge explanations directly in the list.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REPORT\n==================================================\n\nMake Final Report the CLEANEST page in the entire website.\n\nIt should feel like a professional generated environmental verification report.\n\nShow:\n\nCLIMATELENS\nVerification Report\n\nCompany\nReporting Period\n\nKEY METRICS\n\nEmissions\nEnergy\nWater\nWaste\n\nThen:\n\nHistorical Trends\n\nVerification Findings\n\nEnvironmental Context\n\nSource References\n\nReviewer Notes\n\nLimitations\n\nUse graphs heavily but cleanly.\n\nThe final report should summarize the complete workflow without overwhelming the user.\n\nPDF export must use the actual current application state.\n\nCSV export must use the actual current structured data.\n\nDo not generate a fake report disconnected from the application.\n\n==================================================\nHUMAN REVIEW\n==================================================\n\nPreserve the existing human-in-the-loop functionality.\n\nThe AI should assist the reviewer.\n\nThe reviewer should be able to:\n\n- confirm extracted data\n- correct extracted values\n- flag information\n- dismiss a flag\n- add notes\n- verify information\n- view source pages\n- inspect confidence\n- review methodology changes\n\nChanges made by the reviewer must update the relevant application state.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf the reviewer corrects an extracted value:\n\nClean Data\n\342\206\223\nHistorical Analysis\n\342\206\223\nVerification\n\342\206\223\nFlags\n\342\206\223\nFinal Report\n\nshould all reflect the updated value where applicable.\n\n==================================================\nAI / GEMINI\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT remove the existing Gemini integration.\n\nUse Gemini where AI reasoning is actually useful:\n\n- understanding PDF content\n- extracting environmental metrics\n- identifying relevant tables\n- understanding methodology descriptions\n- interpreting reporting boundary changes\n- explaining potential inconsistencies\n- generating concise contextual explanations\n\nUse deterministic code for:\n\n- arithmetic\n- totals\n- percentages\n- unit conversion\n- variance calculations\n- trend calculations\n- comparisons\n- sorting\n- filtering\n- validation rules\n\nDo not ask the AI to perform calculations that should be deterministic.\n\nValidate structured Gemini output before using it.\n\n==================================================\nTRACEABILITY\n==================================================\n\nEvery extracted environmental value should retain, where available:\n\n- source document\n- page\n- section\n- table\n- source text/snippet\n- confidence\n- verification status\n\nThe user should be able to trace:\n\nCLEAN DATA\n\342\206\222 SOURCE\n\342\206\222 ORIGINAL REPORT\n\nDo not remove this functionality during the redesign.\n\n==================================================\nDEMO DATA\n==================================================\n\nIf the existing prototype uses synthetic/demo data, keep it.\n\nClearly label it:\n\nDEMO DATA\nor\nSAMPLE REPORT\n\nDo not present fictional companies or fictional measurements as real-world facts.\n\nThe demo should demonstrate:\n\n- large messy input\n- AI extraction\n- low-confidence extraction\n- standardized values\n- consistency checks\n- historical change\n- environmental context comparison\n- human review\n- final report\n\n==================================================\nIMPORTANT PRODUCT POSITIONING\n==================================================\n\nClimateLens is an AI-integrated environmental verification and intelligence tool.\n\nIt does NOT independently prove that a company has falsified information.\n\nUse:\n\n\"Potential Inconsistency\"\n\n\"Needs Review\"\n\n\"Contextual Difference\"\n\n\"Verification Finding\"\n\n\"Requires Further Verification\"\n\nAvoid:\n\n\"Fraud Detected\"\n\n\"False Data\"\n\n\"Company Lied\"\n\n\"Fake Report\"\n\nunless directly presenting a source\'s claim, and even then clearly attribute it.\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN HIERARCHY\n==================================================\n\nThe final visual hierarchy must be:\n\n1. Overview\n   Clean summary\n\n2. Raw Report\n   Intentionally dense input\n\n3. Processing\n   Transformation\n\n4. Clean Data\n   Structured information\n\n5. Verification\n   Focused findings\n\n6. Environmental Context\n   Visual comparison\n\n7. Final Report\n   Clean professional output\n\nThe user should understand the entire ClimateLens concept simply by moving through the website.\n\n==================================================\nDO NOT BREAK EXISTING CODE\n==================================================\n\nBefore editing:\n\n- inspect existing components\n- inspect existing state\n- inspect existing services\n- inspect existing Gemini implementation\n- inspect existing chart components\n- inspect existing PDF generation\n- inspect existing verification logic\n- inspect existing routes/pages\n\nReuse existing code whenever possible.\n\nDo not duplicate functionality.\n\nDo not create unnecessary files.\n\nDo not replace working logic with placeholder functions.\n\nDo not hard-code the UI to one example.\n\nThe application must continue to work with uploaded reports and its existing data flow.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nThis is a UI/UX REDESIGN OF THE EXISTING CLIMATELENS WEBSITE.\n\nDo not rebuild ClimateLens.\n\nDo not change the project\'s core purpose.\n\nDo not remove working features.\n\nDo not create unrelated features.\n\nMake the existing product:\n\nCLEANER\nSIMPLER\nMORE PROFESSIONAL\nMORE VISUAL\nMORE DATA-DRIVEN\nEASIER TO UNDERSTAND\n\nThe most important visual message must remain:\n\nFROM MESSY REPORTS\nTO\nCLEAR ENVIRONMENTAL DATA."
    items {
      text: "You are modifying an EXISTING website called CLIMATELENS.\n\nIMPORTANT: DO NOT BUILD A NEW WEBSITE.\n\nYour job is ONLY to redesign, reorganize, clean up, and improve the EXISTING ClimateLens website while preserving its existing functionality and project structure.\n\nBefore making ANY changes:\n\n1. Inspect the entire existing project.\n2. Understand the current pages, components, routes, state management, data flow, Gemini/API integration, charts, PDF handling, verification logic, and existing functionality.\n3. Identify which components are already functional.\n4. Reuse existing components and logic wherever possible.\n5. Do not replace working functionality with fake UI or static demonstrations.\n6. Do not remove existing features just because the UI is being redesigned.\n\n==================================================\nCORE PRODUCT IDEA\n==================================================\n\nClimateLens converts complex corporate environmental reports into structured, understandable and reviewable environmental information.\n\nThe visual story of the website must be:\n\nMESSY REPORT\n      \342\206\223\nAI EXTRACTION\n      \342\206\223\nSTANDARDIZATION\n      \342\206\223\nDATA CHECK\n      \342\206\223\nCLEAN DATA\n      \342\206\223\nVERIFICATION\n      \342\206\223\nENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT\n      \342\206\223\nFINAL REPORT\n\nThe website should make this transformation visually obvious.\n\n==================================================\nMOST IMPORTANT DESIGN RULE\n==================================================\n\nTHE WEBSITE ITSELF MUST BE CLEAN.\n\nDo NOT make every page look like a complicated dashboard.\n\nThere should be ONLY ONE intentionally dense area:\n\nRAW REPORT / RAW DATA\n\nThat area should visually represent the huge amount of messy information found inside corporate environmental reports.\n\nEverything after that should progressively become cleaner.\n\nThink:\n\nRAW REPORT = messy and information-heavy\n\nPROCESSING = 