alues\n\nMake this area visually information-heavy.\n\nHowever, it must still be organized and readable.\n\nClearly label demo/sample data if the data is synthetic.\n\nDo NOT make fake data look like real corporate data.\n\n==================================================\nTHE TRANSFORMATION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a strong visual transition from the raw report to the clean dataset.\n\nShow:\n\nRAW REPORT\n\342\206\223\nAI EXTRACTION\n\342\206\223\nSTANDARDIZATION\n\342\206\223\nDATA CHECK\n\342\206\223\nCLEAN DATA\n\nEach step should be visually simple.\n\nFor example:\n\nAI EXTRACTION\nIdentifies environmental values\n\nSTANDARDIZATION\nConverts units and terminology\n\nDATA CHECK\nChecks consistency and completeness\n\nCLEAN DATA\nCreates structured environmental data\n\nDo not use long explanations.\n\n==================================================\nCLEAN DATA\n==================================================\n\nThis section should look dramatically cleaner than Raw Data.\n\nUse a simple structured table.\n\nColumns:\n\nMetric | Value | Unit | Status\n\nExample:\n\nEmissions | 8,420 | tCO\342\202\202e | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nEnergy | 12,450 | MWh | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nWater | 84,200 | KL | \342\234\223 Checked\n\nWaste | 2,840 | tonnes | \342\232\240 Review\n\nUse actual application data where possible.\n\nDo not show every technical field by default.\n\nWhen the user clicks a metric, show additional information:\n\nSource\nPage\nSection\nConfidence\nReporting Period\nMethodology\nReview Status\n\nThis keeps the main table clean while preserving traceability.\n\n==================================================\nGRAPHS\n==================================================\n\nGraphs must become an important visual identity of ClimateLens.\n\nBUT:\n\nDo not add graphs just to make the website look impressive.\n\nEvery graph must communicate useful information.\n\nUse:\n\nLINE GRAPH\nFor historical trends.\n\nBAR GRAPH\nFor category comparisons.\n\nSMALL TREND GRAPH\nFor summary metrics.\n\nCOMPARISON GRAPH\nFor company vs regional environmental indicators.\n\nVERIFICATION GRAPH\nFor highlighting unusual changes.\n\nUse one consistent chart design throughout the application.\n\nDo not use completely different chart styles on every page.\n\nUse the actual application data.\n\n==================================================\nHISTORICAL ANALYSIS\n==================================================\n\nRedesign the existing historical page.\n\nMake the main element a large clean trend graph.\n\nExample:\n\n2023 \342\206\222 2024 \342\206\222 2025 \342\206\222 2026\n\nAllow the user to switch between:\n\n- emissions\n- energy\n- water\n- waste\n- production\n- carbon intensity\n\nIf methodology or reporting boundaries changed, clearly show that as context.\n\nDo not automatically interpret every increase/decrease as suspicious.\n\nUse wording such as:\n\n\"Significant historical change detected.\"\n\nThen explain possible reasons:\n\n- production change\n- reporting boundary change\n- methodology change\n- operational change\n\n==================================================\nVERIFICATION CENTER\n==================================================\n\nKeep the existing verification functionality.\n\nMake the page focused.\n\nShow three simple statuses:\n\n\342\234\223 CONSISTENT\n\n\342\234\223 CHECKED\n\n\342\232\240 NEEDS REVIEW\n\nThen show the most important verification findings.\n\nExample:\n\nScope 1 Emissions\n\n8,420 tCO\342\202\202e\n\nPrevious period:\n6,900 tCO\342\202\202e\n\nChange:\n+22%\n\nStatus:\nNeeds Review\n\nShow the historical graph with the affected point highlighted.\n\nAdd:\n\nINVESTIGATE\n\nWhen clicked, open a side panel/modal.\n\nThe side panel should show:\n\nWhy was this flagged?\n\nWhat values were compared?\n\nWhat source was used?\n\nWhat possible explanations exist?\n\nWhat should the reviewer check?\n\nDo not state that a company has committed fraud or falsified data.\n\nClimateLens identifies potential inconsistencies for human review.\n\n==================================================\nENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nThis page must clearly demonstrate one of ClimateLens\' important capabilities.\n\nShow two trends on the same graph:\n\nCORPORATE TREND\n\nREGIONAL ENVIRONMENTAL INDICATOR\n\nExample:\n\nCompany emissions \342\206\22318%\n\nRegional indicator \342\206\2219%\n\nThen show a concise message:\n\nCONTEXTUAL DIFFERENCE DETECTED\n\n\"Corporate reported emissions moved differently from the selected regional environmental indicator during the comparable period.\"\n\nThen:\n\n\"Further review recommended.\"\n\nAlways include:\n\n\"Correlation does not establish causation.\"\n\nDo not imply that a regional environmental change proves the company\'s reported data is wrong.\n\nKeep this section visual, not text-heavy.\n\n==================================================\nFLAGS\n==================================================\n\nMake Flags very clean.\n\nEach flag should show:\n\nFlag ID\nMetric\nChange\nSeverity\nStatus\n\nExample:\n\nCL-0042\n\nScope 1 Emissions\n\n+22%\n\nNeeds Review\n\nClicking the flag opens detailed information.\n\nDo not show huge explanations directly in the list.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REPORT\n==================================================\n\nMake Final Report the CLEANEST page in the entire website.\n\nIt should feel like a professional generated environmental verification report.\n\nShow:\n\nCLIMATELENS\nVerification Report\n\nCompany\nReporting Period\n\nKEY METRICS\n\nEmissions\nEnergy\nWater\nWaste\n\nThen:\n\nHistorical Trends\n\nVerification Findings\n\nEnvironmental Context\n\nSource References\n\nReviewer Notes\n\nLimitations\n\nUse graphs heavily but cleanly.\n\nThe final report should summarize the complete workflow without overwhelming the user.\n\nPDF export must use the actual current application state.\n\nCSV export must use the actual current structured data.\n\nDo not generate a fake report disconnected from the application.\n\n==================================================\nHUMAN REVIEW\n==================================================\n\nPreserve the existing human-in-the-loop functionality.\n\nThe AI should assist the reviewer.\n\nThe reviewer should be able to:\n\n- confirm extracted data\n- correct extracted values\n- flag information\n- dismiss a flag\n- add notes\n- verify information\n- view source pages\n- inspect confidence\n- review methodology changes\n\nChanges made by the reviewer must update the relevant application state.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf the reviewer corrects an extracted value:\n\nClean Data\n\342\206\223\nHistorical Analysis\n\342\206\223\nVerification\n\342\206\223\nFlags\n\342\206\223\nFinal Report\n\nshould all reflect the updated value where applicable.\n\n==================================================\nAI / GEMINI\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT remove the existing Gemini integration.\n\nUse Gemini where AI reasoning is actually useful:\n\n- understanding PDF content\n- extracting environmental metrics\n- identifying relevant tables\n- understanding methodology descriptions\n- interpreting reporting boundary changes\n- explaining potential inconsistencies\n- generating concise contextual explanations\n\nUse deterministic code for:\n\n- arithmetic\n- totals\n- percentages\n- unit conversion\n- variance calculations\n- trend calculations\n- comparisons\n- sorting\n- filtering\n- validation rules\n\nDo not ask the AI to perform calculations that should be deterministic.\n\nValidate structured Gemini output before using it.\n\n==================================================\nTRACEABILITY\n==================================================\n\nEvery extracted environmental value should retain, where available:\n\n- source document\n- page\n- section\n- table\n- source text/snippet\n- confidence\n- verification status\n\nThe user should be able to trace:\n\nCLEAN DATA\n\342\206\222 SOURCE\n\342\206\222 ORIGINAL REPORT\n\nDo not remove this functionality during the redesign.\n\n==================================================\nDEMO DATA\n==================================================\n\nIf the existing prototype uses synthetic/demo data, keep it.\n\nClearly label it:\n\nDEMO DATA\nor\nSAMPLE REPORT\n\nDo not present fictional companies or fictional measurements as real-world facts.\n\nThe demo should demonstrate:\n\n- large messy input\n- AI extraction\n- low-confidence extraction\n- standardized values\n- consistency checks\n- historical change\n- environmental context comparison\n- human review\n- final report\n\n==================================================\nIMPORTANT PRODUCT POSITIONING\n==================================================\n\nClimateLens is an AI-integrated environmental verification and intelligence tool.\n\nIt does NOT independently prove that a company has falsified information.\n\nUse:\n\n\"Potential Inconsistency\"\n\n\"Needs Review\"\n\n\"Contextual Difference\"\n\n\"Verification Finding\"\n\n\"Requires Further Verification\"\n\nAvoid:\n\n\"Fraud Detected\"\n\n\"False Data\"\n\n\"Company Lied\"\n\n\"Fake Report\"\n\nunless directly presenting a source\'s claim, and even then clearly attribute it.\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN HIERARCHY\n==================================================\n\nThe final visual hierarchy must be:\n\n1. Overview\n   Clean summary\n\n2. Raw Report\n   Intentionally dense input\n\n3. Processing\n   Transformation\n\n4. Clean Data\n   Structured information\n\n5. Verification\n   Focused findings\n\n6. Environmental Context\n   Visual comparison\n\n7. Final Report\n   Clean professional output\n\nThe user should understand the entire ClimateLens concept simply by moving through the website.\n\n==================================================\nDO NOT BREAK EXISTING CODE\n==================================================\n\nBefore editing:\n\n- inspect existing components\n- inspect existing state\n- inspect existing services\n- inspect existing Gemini implementation\n- inspect existing chart components\n- inspect existing PDF generation\n- inspect existing verification logic\n- inspect existing routes/pages\n\nReuse existing code whenever possible.\n\nDo not duplicate functionality.\n\nDo not create unnecessary files.\n\nDo not replace working logic with placeholder functions.\n\nDo not hard-code the UI to one example.\n\nThe application must continue to work with uploaded reports and its existing data flow.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nThis is a UI/UX REDESIGN OF THE EXISTING CLIMATELENS WEBSITE.\n\nDo not rebuild ClimateLens.\n\nDo not change the project\'s core purpose.\n\nDo not remove working features.\n\nDo not create unrelated features.\n\nMake the existing product:\n\nCLEANER\nSIMPLER\nMORE PROFESSIONAL\nMORE VISUAL\nMORE DATA-DRIVEN\nEASIER TO UNDERSTAND\n\nThe most important visual message must remain:\n\nFROM MESSY REPORTS\nTO\nCLEAR ENVIRONMENTAL DATA."
    }
  }
}
